    questions: [
      { id: "a1", q: "You pass your instrument rating test in a multi-engine aeroplane. Next month you want to fly a single-engine C182 under the IFR. What does your multi-engine instrument endorsement cover, and what else must you hold or have current before you go?", hint: "Read column 2 of Table 61.890 for the ME endorsement. Does it say multi-engine aeroplane, or just aeroplane?", answer: "Yes. The multi-engine aeroplane instrument endorsement authorises you to pilot an aeroplane under the IFR or at night under the VFR, so single-engine aeroplanes are included. You still need the class rating, your recency, a valid IPC for the aeroplane category, and the right approach endorsement for the approaches you fly.", ref: "CASR 61.865, Table 61.890 item 2, 61.880" },
      { id: "a2", q: "Your rating test included a circling approach. Thirteen months later you haven't done an IPC since. What are the three ways to hold circling privileges, and which of them, if any, still applies to you?", hint: "There are three ways to hold circling privileges. Which of them still applies after 12 months?", answer: "The three ways: you passed the rating flight test within the previous 12 months and it included a circling approach; your most recent IPC (including an OPC covering IFR run by an examiner with an IR flight test endorsement) included a circling approach; or you're successfully participating in an approved operator training and checking system that covers circling. None applies after 13 months with no IPC, so you need an IPC that includes a circling approach before you plan one.", ref: "CASR 61.860(3)–(4)" },
      { id: "a3", q: "Every approach you've flown in training has been an RNP or an ILS. After you get your rating, what must you do before you can fly an NDB approach in IMC, and which regulations require it?", hint: "Holding the 2D endorsement is one condition. What does 61.860(5) add?", answer: "Three things. Complete training in NDB approaches and demonstrate competence to CASA, an examiner authorised for that procedure, or an approved person (61.860(5)–(6)). Have previously flown an approach, or a simulated one in an FSTD, using that kind of navigation system before doing it in IMC (61.900(1)). And be recent: an azimuth guidance operation and a 2D operation in the previous 90 days, on top of the 3-approach rule (61.870).", ref: "CASR 61.860(5)–(6), 61.900(1), 61.870(4), (6)" },
      { id: "a4", q: "What are the two privileges of an instrument rating?", hint: "One is IFR. The other happens after last light.", answer: "Piloting an aircraft under the IFR, and piloting an aircraft at night under the VFR. Both are subject to the endorsement, recency and IPC conditions in 61.860 to 61.880.", ref: "CASR 61.855" },
      { id: "a5", q: "You hold a CPL with a multi-engine instrument rating. An operator asks you to fly a passenger charter under the IFR in a single-engine C182. What does Part 135 require of the aeroplane, and how would the answer change for a private flight?", hint: "135.230: which aeroplanes can carry passengers under the IFR?", answer: "Part 135 IFR flights must be in a multi-engine aeroplane or a prescribed single-engine aeroplane (PSEA). A C182 isn't a PSEA, so the charter can't be flown IFR in it. Privately under Part 91 there's no such restriction: your ME instrument endorsement covers any aeroplane, so with a class rating, recency and a valid IPC you could fly it IFR.", ref: "CASR 135.230, 135.240" },
      { id: "a6", q: "An operator wants you as PIC of a light twin (under 5,700 kg) on a night IFR passenger charter. You have 12 hours multi-engine, 11 hours on type and 8 hours IFR at night. Work through the Part 135 PIC hours table: which rows do you meet, and which don't you?", hint: "Check each row of the PIC experience table.", answer: "No. Multi-engine (10 hours) and type (10 hours) are met, but IFR at night needs 15 hours as PIC or PICUS under the IFR at night, and you have 8. The operator's exposition may also set higher minimums.", ref: "CASR 135.395(1)(b) table" },
      { id: "a7", q: "You're flying a pipeline survey under Part 138, then flying home IFR afterwards. Which rules apply to the flight home?", hint: "What does AC 138-01 say about flights with no aerial work?", answer: "Part 91 in full. Part 138 relief (such as the minimum height rules) only applies while you're doing the aerial work under the operator's risk assessment. Ferry and positioning flights must comply fully with Part 91.", ref: "AC 138-01 2.4.4; CASR 138.375" },
      { id: "a8", q: "Your rating test is flown single-pilot in the Seminole. Under 61.860(2), what must you have done to fly single-pilot IFR, and how would it differ if your test had been flown in a multi-crew aircraft?", hint: "It's about the kind of aircraft the test or IPC was in.", answer: "Single-pilot IFR is only authorised if you passed the rating flight test in a single-pilot aircraft or completed an IPC in a single-pilot aircraft. Your Seminole test meets that. Had the test been multi-crew only, you couldn't fly single-pilot IFR until you'd completed an IPC in a single-pilot aircraft. Single-pilot recency under 61.875 applies on top.", ref: "CASR 61.860(2), 61.875" },
      { id: "a9", q: "You hold the IAP 3D endorsement. List every condition you must meet before flying an ILS in IMC as PIC of the Seminole.", hint: "Aircraft equipment, training in the procedure, prior use of the navigation system, recency, 3D currency, IPC.", answer: "The aircraft must be equipped for that kind of approach (61.860(1)). You must have completed training in the procedure and demonstrated competence to CASA, an examiner or an approved person (61.860(5)–(6)). You must have previously flown an approach (or a simulated one in an FSTD) using that kind of navigation system before doing it in IMC (61.900(1)). You need 3 approaches, a 3D and a CDI operation in the previous 90 days (61.870). Your 3D privileges must be current under 61.900(2). And you need a valid multi-engine IPC (61.880).", ref: "CASR 61.860(1), (5)–(6); 61.870; 61.880; 61.900(1)–(2)" },
      { id: "a10", q: "Your rating test on 24 November 2026 includes an ILS. Your 2027 IPC includes only 2D approaches. In April 2028, what has to be true before you fly an ILS?", hint: "61.900(2) has three ways to keep 3D privileges. Which still works once the test is more than 12 months ago?", answer: "The 12-month window from the test has closed. Because your most recent IPC didn't include a 3D approach, you must have demonstrated 3D competence to CASA, an examiner authorised for that procedure, or an approved person within the previous 15 months. By April 2028 the test is more than 15 months ago, so you need a fresh 3D demonstration (for example, an IPC that includes one) first, unless you're in an approved training and checking system that covers 3D approaches.", ref: "CASR 61.900(2)–(4)" },
      { id: "a11", q: "How do the privileges and limits of a private instrument rating compare with your instrument rating?", hint: "Look at 61.910 to 61.920: kind of operation, aircraft, night and recency.", answer: "A private instrument rating only covers private operations in Australian territory, in aircraft certificated for single-pilot operation with MTOW not more than 5,700 kg. Night IFR needs a night private instrument endorsement, and an approach in IMC needs one of the same kind in the previous 6 months. Your instrument rating has none of those operation, aircraft or territory limits, adds night VFR, and runs on 90-day recency and an annual IPC.", ref: "CASR 61.855, 61.910, 61.915(3), 61.920(1)" },
    ],
