    questions: [
      { id: "c1", q: "It's 15 February 2027. Your last approaches were an RNP and an ILS on 10 November 2026. Which IFR recency requirements do you fail, and how would you get current again?", hint: "Count the days, then count the approaches.", answer: "You fail the lot. 10 November to 15 February is more than 90 days, so you have no approaches in the window, let alone the 3 needed to fly IFR at all, the category approach, or the 2D, 3D and CDI approaches needed for those kinds. Get current by flying approaches in an approved FSTD, under the VFR in VMC by day, or with a current instructor or examiner as PIC.", ref: "CASR 61.870(2)–(7)" },
      { id: "c2", q: "In the last 90 days you've flown three RNP approaches and nothing else. Which kinds of approach can you legally fly today?", hint: "An RNP to LNAV minima is 2D and uses a CDI. What's missing?", answer: "Approaches that are 2D CDI operations, such as RNP to LNAV minima, VOR and LOC. You can't fly a 3D approach like an ILS (no 3D in 90 days) or an azimuth guidance approach like an NDB (no azimuth operation in 90 days).", ref: "CASR 61.870(4)–(7)" },
      { id: "c3", q: "You meet the 90-day rules, but every flight in the last 7 months has been in a multi-crew setting. What exactly does 61.875 require for single-pilot IFR, and do you meet it?", hint: "61.875 is separate from 61.870.", answer: "61.875 needs a single-pilot IFR flight or simulated flight in the previous 6 months, lasting at least 1 hour and including at least 1 instrument approach (or simulated approach). With 7 months of multi-crew only, you don't meet it, so no single-pilot IFR until you've done one.", ref: "CASR 61.875" },
      { id: "c4", q: "Which IFR recency requirements can you meet in a simulator, and what kind of simulator must it be?", hint: "Read the end of each subregulation.", answer: "All of them. Every 61.870 requirement (3 approaches, category, 2D, 3D, azimuth guidance, CDI) can be met in an aircraft or an approved flight simulation training device for the purpose, and 61.875 single-pilot recency accepts a simulated flight. A home simulator that isn't approved doesn't count.", ref: "CASR 61.870, 61.875" },
      { id: "c5", q: "You're not IFR-recent. How can you legally get current again?", hint: "61.870 limits flying under the IFR. Where can approaches be flown without being IFR?", answer: "Fly the approaches in an approved FSTD, or fly them in an aircraft under the VFR in VMC by day (61.870 only stops you piloting under the IFR), or fly with an instructor or examiner who is current and acting as pilot in command. Then you can fly IFR yourself.", ref: "CASR 61.870" },
      { id: "c6", q: "You fly IFR charter and passed your operator's IFR proficiency check 2 months ago, but you've flown only 1 approach in the last 90 days. Which recency rules apply to you, and which don't?", hint: "61.870(1A).", answer: "Yes, for the 61.870 recency. It doesn't apply if you've passed an operator proficiency check covering IFR in the previous 3 months, or you're in an approved training and checking system. Single-pilot recency under 61.875 and the operator's rules still apply.", ref: "CASR 61.870(1A)" },
      { id: "c7", q: "For 61.870 recency, classify each of these: an NDB approach, a VOR approach, a LOC approach, an ILS, and an RNP to LNAV minima flown using the altitude/distance scale. Which recency boxes does each tick?", hint: "Each approach is 2D or 3D, and it's either azimuth guidance or a CDI operation.", answer: "NDB: 2D and azimuth guidance (bearing). VOR: 2D and CDI. LOC: 2D and CDI. ILS: 3D and CDI. RNP flown with the altitude/distance scale: 2D and CDI. All of them count towards the 3 approaches in 90 days and the category approach.", ref: "CASR 61.010, 61.870(2)–(7); AIP GEN 2.2" },
      { id: "c8", q: "When does an NPA count as a 3D instrument approach operation rather than 2D, and why are your Seminole RNP approaches normally 2D?", hint: "Look at how the AIP classifies a CDFA with on-board advisory vertical guidance, then at the POH on SBAS.", answer: "The AIP classes an NPA flown as a CDFA with advisory vertical guidance calculated by on-board equipment as a 3D operation; flown using the charted altitude/distance scale, it's 2D. The POH says G1000 GPS vertical guidance isn't available without SBAS corrections, and the AIP says no SBAS signal is to be used in Australia. So your Seminole RNPs are 2D, and an ILS gives you 3D recency. Confirm with your examiner how they treat it.", ref: "AIP GEN 2.2 (Instrument Approach Operations), ENR 1.5 2.4.4, ENR 4.3; POH 2.27(f)(1)" },
      { id: "c9", q: "Your last three approaches were an ILS on 1 December, an RNP on 20 December and an NDB on 10 January. Roughly when does each kind of recency lapse, and when can you no longer fly IFR at all?", hint: "Count 90 days from each approach. What happens to the 3-approach rule when the first one drops out?", answer: "3D lapses around 1 March, when the ILS drops out. From then you have only 2 approaches in the window, so you can't fly IFR at all. Without that, CDI and 2D would have lasted to about 20 March (the RNP) and azimuth guidance to about 10 April (the NDB), but the 3-approach rule ends everything on about 1 March.", ref: "CASR 61.870(2)–(7)" },
      { id: "c10", q: "The day after your test, list the IPC, IFR recency, single-pilot recency and night passenger recency you'll hold, and roughly when each lapses if you fly nothing more.", hint: "Test on 24 November 2026: 12 months, 90 days, 6 months, 90 days.", answer: "IPC: to the end of November 2027. IFR recency: about 22 February 2027, provided the test included at least 3 approaches of the kinds you want to fly. Single-pilot recency: about 24 May 2027 (6 months, test over 1 hour with an approach). Night passenger recency: about 22 February 2027, provided the test included a night take-off and landing. The approach-kind recencies depend on which approaches you flew in the test.", ref: "CASR 61.880(3)(a), 61.870, 61.875, 61.395(5)" },
    ],
